<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Amiens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1802</span> · Also known as Peace of Amiens · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, signed in March 1802 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, briefly ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. Britain returned most of its conquests but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, taken from Spain, and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> possessions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. The peace collapsed in May 1803, when Britain declared war again, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens.jpg" alt="Treaty of Amiens" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules-Claude Ziegler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Paix_d%27Amiens_de_Jules-Claude_Ziegler_(1853).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
